---
/**
 * One week of the schedule as a compact band of day cells, for the homepage.
 *
 * Deliberately NOT `DayRow`. That component is the full record of a day and is
 * what `/program/` renders; this one is the glance — the question "when is
 * Sunday" answered without scrolling. So it drops the month name, the day's
 * `notes` and the cancelled-day sentence, and keeps only what decides whether
 * someone comes: the date, the times, the feast, the fast, and the location on
 * the rare day that has one.
 *
 * `notes` is the one field of the day that reaches the `.ics` DESCRIPTION and
 * does not appear here. That is a summary dropping detail, not a page
 * contradicting the feed: `/program/` carries it, and "Programul complet →"
 * sits directly under this band. `location` is different in kind and is
 * rendered — see below.
 *
 * Every colour comes from a token in `global.css`; a hex literal anywhere in
 * this file fails `stylesheet.itest.ts` against `dist`.
 */
import type { Week } from '../lib/schedule';
import { serviceLabel, fullStop } from '../lib/schedule';
import { dayName, dayOfMonth } from '../lib/date-ro';

interface Props { week: Week }
const { week } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="bs">
  {week.days.map((z) => (
    <div class:list={['bs-zi', (z.great_feast || z.feast) && 'bs-praznic', z.cancelled && 'bs-anulat']}>
      <div class="bs-cap">
        <span class="bs-nume">{dayName(z.date)}</span>
        <span class="bs-nr">{dayOfMonth(z.date)}</span>
      </div>
      {/*
        Above the times and suppressed on a cancelled day, both for the reasons
        `DayRow` spells out: the address changes how the times should be read,
        and "Anulat" followed by "Slujbele au loc la Winterthur." is a flat
        contradiction with nothing to mark which sentence wins.

        It is here at all because `ics.ts` writes `z.location || opts.location`
        into LOCATION. A homepage that omitted it would send the person who
        reads the page to the usual building while the person subscribed to the
        feed went to the right one. Shorter than `/program/`'s wording by the
        two words the column heading already supplies ("acestei zile"), because
        the cell is ~10rem wide.

        `fullStop` rather than a literal ".": the schema keeps the field
        exactly as the parish typed it, so an editor who ended it with a stop
        would otherwise read "Winterthur..". The same call is in `DayRow`.
      */}
      {!z.cancelled && z.location && (
        <p class="bs-loc">Slujbele au loc la {z.location}{fullStop(z.location)}</p>
      )}
      {/*
        A cancelled day keeps its times, struck through, exactly as `/program/`
        renders it. The band used to replace them with the word "Anulat", and
        two pages describing the same day in different shapes is how a reader
        decides one of them is stale.

        The sentence carries the fact and the line-through only illustrates it:
        `text-decoration` never reaches the accessibility tree, so a screen
        reader hears the times and nothing else unless a sentence says so. That
        is also why the sentence comes FIRST — it has to be heard before the
        times it applies to, not after them.

        Abbreviated from `/program/` by the same two words `.bs-loc` drops, so
        the band's two exception sentences stay parallel to the page's two:
        "Slujbele acestei zile sunt anulate." → "Slujbele sunt anulate."
      */}
      {z.cancelled && <p class="bs-anulat-txt">Slujbele sunt anulate.</p>}
      {z.services.map((s) => (
        /*
          The explicit space is load-bearing, and `margin-right` below is not a
          substitute for it. JSX puts no whitespace between `</b>` and the next
          expression, so without it the accessibility tree and a copy-paste both
          read `07:30Utrenia` — which looks perfect on screen, because the margin
          draws the gap the space does not. Exactly the shape Task 8 fixed
          between a feast name and its fast tag: no screenshot and no axe run can
          see it, a screen reader says it. Measured on the built page:
          `innerText` is "07:30 Utrenia", and the visible gap is 9.65px — 6.4px
          of margin plus a 3.25px space.
        */
        <p class="bs-slujba"><b>{s.time}</b>{' '}{serviceLabel(s)}</p>
      ))}
      {z.feast && <p class="bs-praznic-txt">{z.great_feast && <span aria-hidden="true">† </span>}{z.feast}</p>}
      {z.fast_day && <p class="bs-post">Zi de post</p>}
    </div>
  ))}
</div>

<style>
  /* `auto-fit` with a 10rem minimum, measured rather than assumed. At 375px the
     container is 343px, so the band lays out TWO columns of 171.5px — not the
     one column the plan's step 3 predicted, which would need a viewport under
     352px. Two were kept because a cell was measured against what it must hold,
     which is the check DayRow's fixed date track failed:

       content box                     143.0px  (171 client − 2 × 14 padding)
       widest day name  DUMINICĂ        64.4px  (10px, uppercase, 0.18em)
       widest service word  Spovedanie  65.4px  (13px)

     Both clear by more than half the box, `scrollWidth === clientWidth` on every
     cell, and the document does not scroll sideways at 375px. `1fr` as the
     maximum is what keeps that true: the track grows with the container, so the
     10rem is only ever a minimum and can never decide whether a word fits.

     The one shape this does NOT flatter is a seven-day week on a wide screen:
     1016px of container fits six 10rem tracks, so Holy Week wraps 6 + 1 and
     leaves Sunday alone on a second row. Measured, not guessed; see the report. */
  .bs { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(10rem, 1fr)); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .bs-zi { padding: 0.875rem 0.875rem 1.125rem; border-right: 1px solid var(--rule); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  /* Feast days are marked by background AND a rule, never by colour alone.
     Flat, not a gradient: axe reports contrast over a gradient as `incomplete`,
     which `npm run a11y` treats as a failure. Same ruling as `DayRow`. */
  .bs-praznic { background: var(--raised); box-shadow: inset 0 2px 0 var(--gold-lt); }
  .bs-nume { font-family: var(--display); font-size: 0.625rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--faint); display: block; }
  .bs-nr { font-family: var(--display); font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.05; color: var(--oxblood); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .bs-slujba { margin: 0; font-size: 0.8125rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .bs-slujba b { color: var(--gold-text); font-weight: 600; margin-right: 0.4rem; }
  .bs-praznic-txt { font-family: var(--display); font-style: italic; font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--oxblood); margin: 0.4rem 0 0; }
  .bs-post { font-size: 0.5625rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--oxblood); border: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 0.05rem 0.35rem; display: inline-block; margin: 0.4rem 0 0; }
  /* A cancelled day is marked by the sentence above and by a line through its
     times — deliberately NOT by dimming the cell, which is the obvious thing to
     reach for and the one this palette has no room for. `opacity` below 1 blends
     text toward the page, and `--muted` leaves AA at 0.879 on the page and 0.860
     on a feast cell: that is a dimming anyone would actually use, not a rounding
     error. `DayRow` measured the same trap at `opacity: .75` and got 2.96:1.
     `--gold-lt` is out for the other reason — 2.18:1 on this ground. At full
     strength `--muted` is 5.90:1 on the page and 6.25:1 on a feast cell.

     One selector rather than `DayRow`'s two: there the time and the label are
     grid items, and `text-decoration` does not propagate into those. Here the
     label is a text node inside the `<p>`, so the decoration reaches both it and
     the `<b>`. Verified in a browser, not assumed — see the report. */
  .bs-anulat-txt { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--muted); }
  .bs-anulat .bs-slujba { text-decoration: line-through; }
  .bs-loc { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--muted); }
  /* Seven days, kept on one row, because the week that has seven is Holy Week —
     the most services, the most visitors, and a Sunday that is Easter. At the
     base 10rem minimum the 1016px container fits only six tracks, so that week
     wrapped 6 + 1 and stranded Easter on a row of its own.

     A breakpoint rather than a smaller minimum everywhere, so this buys the
     desktop case without spending the phone one. Lowering the base to 8rem would
     also fix the wrap, and would drag the 3-column threshold down with it: the
     band goes to three columns once the content box reaches 3 × the minimum,
     which is 522px of viewport today — measured, 2 columns at 520px and 3 at
     522px — and would be about 418px at 8rem. Large phones in landscape would
     get three 130px columns instead of two 171.5px ones. Below 62rem this rule
     does not apply and nothing changes at all.

     62rem (992px) is the narrowest viewport where seven tracks still hold their
     content: the container is 920px there, so each cell is 131.4px with a
     103.4px content box, against a widest day name of 64.4px and a widest
     service word of 65.4px. Above 68rem the container caps at 1016px, which is
     seven tracks and never eight (8 × 128 = 1024 > 1016), so a five-day week
     still renders as five equal columns — `auto-fit` collapses the two spare
     tracks and `1fr` redistributes. Measured at 992, 1088, 1280 and 1440. */
  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .bs { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(8rem, 1fr)); }
  }
</style>
